A level-order walk makes it easy to compare corresponding positions. Each queue entry keeps one node from each tree. Missing children are kept in the pair, so a missing node on only one side is detected right away.

The code returns true only after every pair has matched. It does not modify either tree.

```go
/**
 * Definition for a binary tree node.
 * type TreeNode struct {
 *     Data int
 *     Left *TreeNode
 *     Right *TreeNode
 * }
 */

type nodePair struct {
    first  *TreeNode
    second *TreeNode
}

func IsSameTree(p *TreeNode, q *TreeNode) bool {
    queue := []nodePair{{first: p, second: q}}
    index := 0

    for index < len(queue) {
        pair := queue[index]
        index++

        if pair.first == nil && pair.second == nil {
            continue
        }
        if pair.first == nil || pair.second == nil {
            return false
        }
        if pair.first.Data != pair.second.Data {
            return false
        }

        queue = append(queue,
            nodePair{first: pair.first.Left, second: pair.second.Left},
            nodePair{first: pair.first.Right, second: pair.second.Right},
        )
    }

    return true
}
```

Dry run: with matching roots, the left and right pairs are appended. If the second tree has a missing right child while the first has one, that pair contains one nil pointer and returns false.

Time complexity: `O(n)`.
Space complexity: `O(w)` for the queue, where `w` is the widest level.

An explicit stack avoids recursion. Each stack item contains nodes that should be at the same position in the two trees. After comparing them, their left pair and right pair are added for later checks.

This is useful when a very deep tree could make recursive calls too large. The stack can still grow to the height of the tree, but the input structure stays unchanged.

```cpp
/**
 * Definition for a binary tree node.
 * struct TreeNode {
 *     int data;
 *     TreeNode *left;
 *     TreeNode *right;
 *      TreeNode(int val) : data(val) , left(nullptr) , right(nullptr) {}
 * };
 **/

#include <stack>
#include <utility>

class Solution {
public:
    bool isSameTree(TreeNode* p, TreeNode* q) {
        std::stack<std::pair<TreeNode*, TreeNode*>> nodes;
        nodes.push({p, q});

        while (!nodes.empty()) {
            auto current = nodes.top();
            nodes.pop();

            TreeNode* first = current.first;
            TreeNode* second = current.second;

            if (first == nullptr && second == nullptr) {
                continue;
            }
            if (first == nullptr || second == nullptr) {
                return false;
            }
            if (first->data != second->data) {
                return false;
            }

            nodes.push({first->left, second->left});
            nodes.push({first->right, second->right});
        }

        return true;
    }
};
```

A dry run with roots `5` and `5` pushes the two left nodes and two right nodes. The loop keeps checking matching positions until a mismatch is found or the stack becomes empty.

Time complexity: `O(n)`.
Space complexity: `O(h)` in the usual balanced or one-sided tree cases, based on the stored traversal path.

This version checks the trees level by level. A queue stores one node from each tree as a pair, so matching positions are always compared together.

The queue stores pairs instead of putting null values directly into a Java `ArrayDeque`, since that queue does not accept null elements. The input trees are only read, not changed.

```java
/**
 * Definition for a binary tree node.
 * public class TreeNode {
 *     int data;
 *     TreeNode left;
 *     TreeNode right;
 *     TreeNode(int val) { data = val; left = null; right = null; }
 * }
 **/

import java.util.ArrayDeque;
import java.util.Queue;

class Solution {
    private static class NodePair {
        TreeNode first;
        TreeNode second;

        NodePair(TreeNode first, TreeNode second) {
            this.first = first;
            this.second = second;
        }
    }

    public boolean isSameTree(TreeNode p, TreeNode q) {
        Queue<NodePair> queue = new ArrayDeque<>();
        queue.offer(new NodePair(p, q));

        while (!queue.isEmpty()) {
            NodePair pair = queue.poll();
            TreeNode first = pair.first;
            TreeNode second = pair.second;

            if (first == null && second == null) {
                continue;
            }
            if (first == null || second == null) {
                return false;
            }
            if (first.data != second.data) {
                return false;
            }

            queue.offer(new NodePair(first.left, second.left));
            queue.offer(new NodePair(first.right, second.right));
        }

        return true;
    }
}
```

The empty-tree case is covered by the first pair in the queue. If both roots are null, the loop finishes and returns true.

Time complexity: `O(n)`.
Space complexity: `O(w)`, where `w` is the largest level width.

