```java[]
/**
 * Definition for a binary tree node.
 * public class TreeNode {
 *     int data;
 *     TreeNode left;
 *     TreeNode right;
 *     TreeNode(int val) { data = val; left = null, right = null }
 * }
 **/

class Solution {
    public boolean isSameTree(TreeNode p, TreeNode q) {
        boolean ans = true;
        if(p != q && (p == null || q == null)) return false;
        else if(p == q) return true;
        Queue<TreeNode> P = new LinkedList<>();
        Queue<TreeNode> Q = new LinkedList<>();
        P.add(p); Q.add(q);
        while(!P.isEmpty() || !Q.isEmpty()) {
            if(P.isEmpty() || Q.isEmpty()) return false;
            p = P.remove();
            q = Q.remove();
            if( (p != q && (p == null || q == null)) 
            || ( p != null && q!=null && p.data != q.data)) {
               return false;
            }
            if(p!=null) {
                P.add(p.left);  
                P.add(p.right); 
            } 
            if(q!=null) {
                Q.add(q.left);  
                Q.add(q.right); 
            } 
        }
        return true;
    }
}
```

## Complexity
**Time Complexity:** 
O(N) where N is the total number of nodes in both trees combined, as each node is visited and added to the queue exactly once.

**Space Complexity:**
O(W) where W is the maximum width of the tree, representing the maximum number of nodes stored in the queues at any level.
